====================================================================================================
C. Petersen = hemi-dodecahedron; map group
====================================================================================================
  dodecahedron: V=20 E=30 F=12; antipodal map = unique vertex at distance 5, an automorphism, maps faces to faces
  quotient: V=10 E=15, 6 face pentagons, isomorphic to Petersen: True; chi = 1 (projective plane)
  |Aut(Petersen graph)| = 120; automorphisms preserving the 6 hemi-dodecahedral faces: 60
  flags: 60; orbit of one flag under the 60 map automorphisms: 60 (regular action)
  element orders of the map group: {1: 1, 2: 15, 3: 20, 5: 24}  (A_5: {1: 1, 2: 15, 3: 20, 5: 24})
  hemi-icosahedron (the other (2,3,5) quotient): K6 is non-planar too: planar(K6) = False

====================================================================================================
D. the Klein-four Belyi map and X(4) -> X(2)
====================================================================================================
  lambda = (t - 1)**2*(t + 1)**2 / (t**2 + 1)**2 ; lambda - 1 = -4*t**2 / (t**2 + 1)**2
  at t = oo (t = 1/s): lambda = (s - 1)**2*(s + 1)**2/(s**2 + 1)**2 ; lambda - 1 = -4*s**2/(s**2 + 1)**2
  fibre over 0: {1: 2, -1: 2} ; over 1: {0: 2} plus t = oo (order of vanishing of lambda-1 at s=0: 2) ; over oo: {-I: 2, I: 2}
  finite critical points (zeros of lambda'): {-1: 1, 1: 1, 0: 1}
  deck group {t, -t, 1/t, -1/t} = Z/2 x Z/2 of order 4 = degree -> Galois; R-H: 2*0-2 = 4*(2*0-2) + 6 -> True
  numerical monodromy on the fibre over 1/2 (4 points): {'around 0': [1, 0, 3, 2], 'around 1': [3, 2, 1, 0]}
  monodromy group order 4, all non-identity elements involutions without fixed points: True
  theta check at tau = (0.137 + 1.21j): |lambda(t(tau)) - lambda(tau)| = 1.98e-31
    gamma = (1, 4, 0, 1)       in +-Gamma(4): True   t(gamma tau) = t   (residual 3.4e-31)
    gamma = (1, 0, 4, 1)       in +-Gamma(4): True   t(gamma tau) = t   (residual 2.4e-31)
    gamma = (5, -4, 4, -3)     in +-Gamma(4): True   t(gamma tau) = t   (residual 2.3e-30)
    gamma = (-3, 4, -4, 5)     in +-Gamma(4): True   t(gamma tau) = t   (residual 1.1e-30)
    gamma = (1, -4, 4, -15)    in +-Gamma(4): True   t(gamma tau) = t   (residual 2.9e-30)
    gamma = (5, 4, 16, 13)     in +-Gamma(4): True   t(gamma tau) = t   (residual 7.7e-31)
    gamma = (1, 2, 0, 1)       in +-Gamma(4): False  t(gamma tau) = 1/t   (residual 1.0e-30)
    gamma = (1, 0, 2, 1)       in +-Gamma(4): False  t(gamma tau) = -t   (residual 1.1e-31)
    gamma = (3, 2, 4, 3)       in +-Gamma(4): False  t(gamma tau) = 1/t   (residual 1.9e-30)
    gamma = (-1, 2, -2, 3)     in +-Gamma(4): False  t(gamma tau) = -1/t   (residual 2.3e-30)
  -> t is a Hauptmodul for Gamma(4) and Gamma(2)/(+-Gamma(4)) acts as t -> +-t^(+-1): the Klein-four Belyi map
     is the modular cover X(4) -> X(2) (also forced: F_2 = Gamma(2)-bar has a unique normal subgroup with quotient V_4)

====================================================================================================
E. Pythagorean Frey curves y^2 = x(x - a^2)(x + b^2): 4-torsion, full torsion, and the Kubert family
====================================================================================================
  order-4 point (a(a+c), ac(a+c)) with 2Q = (a^2, 0): verified for 6560 (triple, leg order) pairs (depth <= 7)
  pairs whose curve has a halvable point of order 4 (torsion Z/2 x Z/8): 0 []
    (3,4,5): gcd of #E(F_p) over good p < 400 = 8 (torsion order divides it)
    (5,12,13): gcd of #E(F_p) over good p < 400 = 8 (torsion order divides it)
    (21,20,29): gcd of #E(F_p) over good p < 400 = 8 (torsion order divides it)
    (15,8,17): gcd of #E(F_p) over good p < 400 = 8 (torsion order divides it)
    (7,24,25): gcd of #E(F_p) over good p < 400 = 8 (torsion order divides it)
    (55,48,73): gcd of #E(F_p) over good p < 400 = 8 (torsion order divides it)
  y^2 = x(x+1)(x+4): (0,0) halvable (0-(-1)=1, 0-(-4)=4 squares) -> Z/2 x Z/4 subset of E(Q);
    its six lambda-values [Fraction(-3, 1), Fraction(-1, 3), Fraction(1, 4), Fraction(3, 4), Fraction(4, 3), Fraction(4, 1)]; values with lambda and 1-lambda both squares: [] -> NOT a Pythagorean Frey curve
  So the Pythagorean Frey curves are the thin subfamily lambda, 1-lambda both squares = image of X(4)(Q),
  i.e. a degree-2 image inside the Z/2 x Z/4 family (X_1(2,4) = X(Gamma(2) cap Gamma_1(4))); over Q(i) they have full 4-torsion.

